 1. Diagnose — Find the actual knowledge gap.
Before explaining anything, determine what the person already understands and exactly where they're getting lost. This prevents you from over-explaining or starting at the wrong level.

2. Deconstruct — Break the concept into its essential components.  
Strip away unnecessary technical detail. Identify the few pieces someone actually needs to understand before the whole concept makes sense.

3. Sequence — Put those pieces in the right order.  
Explain prerequisite concepts first. Each new piece should build logically on the previous one. Don't introduce concept C until A and B make sense.

4. Ground — Make the abstract concrete.  
Use an example, analogy, actual numbers, or the person's own situation. This is where something theoretical becomes tangible.

5. Verify — Test understanding, don't assume it.
Instead of simply asking _“Does that make sense?”_, ask a question that requires them to apply what you explained. If they can't, identify where the chain broke and go back to that step.
